# Superpowers model selection

When Superpowers (SDD, parallel agents, reviews) or the Task tool
dispatches a subagent, **always set `model` explicitly**. Never omit it
and never use `inherit` — that silently uses the parent session model,
often an expensive flagship.

This rule does not change the chat model picked in the Cursor UI. It
only binds Superpowers / Task dispatches.

## Default

Use **Grok 4.6 extra high (Fast Mode)** (`cursor-grok-4.6-xhigh-fast`)
for implementers, reviewers, explore, general-purpose, and specialized
reviewers (`tauri-reviewer`, `ui-kit-reviewer`).

## Cheap alternatives

If a different model is needed, prefer cheaper enabled options, in this
order:

1. Kimi K3 (`kimi-k3-max`)
2. GLM 5.2 (`glm-5.2-high`)
3. Other cheap enabled models (Composer 2.5 Fast, Gemini 3.7 Flash, …)

Any enabled Cursor model is allowed. This is a preference order, not a
ban list. GPT 5.6 (sol/luna/terra) is allowed if enabled, but is not a
default.

## When to elevate

Pick the model during **planning and orchestration**, before dispatch.
Choose the cheapest model that can do that task. Do not start on
flagship by default.

Use **Sonnet 5** (`claude-sonnet-5-thinking-high`) first, then **Opus 5**
(`claude-opus-5-thinking-high`) when either:

1. Planning or orchestration decides Grok / Kimi / GLM are not enough
   (architecture, high-risk security, subtle concurrency, multi-crate
   design).
2. A cheaper subagent gets stuck or cannot finish — re-dispatch on a
   more capable expensive model. Do not retry the same cheap model with
   the same prompt.

Prefer Sonnet 5 over Opus 5 when either would do.

## Reviews

Superpowers’ “final review = most capable” default does **not** apply.
Whole-branch review, Bugbot, and Security Review stay on Grok unless
planning classified the review as needing a flagship model, or a cheaper
reviewer got stuck. Do not spend Opus on a mechanical spec check.
